  <section>
    <h2>Can you trust cheap Premium from other bots?</h2>
    <p>Only after checking. Many bots sell Premium; some are honest, some are scams — and the scammers' main bait is a <strong>price far below the market</strong>. An honest seller pays for Premium itself, so it can't sell below its own cost. If 12 months are offered at a half or a third of the usual price, something is wrong. Warning signs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>They ask for your password, SMS code or 2FA password.</strong> None of these is needed to activate Premium.</li>
      <li><strong>They want to log in to your account.</strong> "We'll log in and switch Premium on for you" is the fastest way to lose your account.</li>
      <li><strong>Prepayment, then silence.</strong> "Pay first" in a private chat, then no reply or a block.</li>
      <li><strong>The price exists only in chat.</strong> No public price page, a different price for everyone.</li>
      <li><strong>No written terms.</strong> No public offer, refund procedure or delivery time.</li>
      <li><strong>"Free Premium" links.</strong> Click a link or enter a code for "free Premium" — classic phishing.</li>
    </ul>
